A null pointer exception is a runtime exception that occurs when you try to access or manipulate a null (non-existent) object in your code.

A null pointer is an object that has no value or no reference to any object. It's essentially an empty object.

Here's an example in Java:

```java
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String str = null;
        System.out.println(str.length()); // This will throw a NullPointerException
    }
}
```

In this example, `str` is a null reference because it's not assigned any value. When you try to access `str.length()`, you're trying to access a non-existent object.

To avoid this, you should always check if a variable is null before trying to access its properties or methods. Here's how you can do it in Java:

```java
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String str = null;
        if (str != null) {
            System.out.println(str.length()); // This will work
        } else {
            System.out.println("String is null");
        }
    }
}
```

In this example, we're checking if `str` is not null before trying to access its properties. If `str` is null, we print a message saying "String is null".

Remember, null pointer exceptions can be very frustrating to deal with, but they're a part of the Java programming language.
